steps.body:
An index loop counts `i` from 0 up to the size and reads each element with `v[i]`. Use it when you need the position, not just the value.

```cpp
for (std::size_t i = 0; i < v.size(); ++i) {
    std::cout << i << ": " << v[i] << '\n';
}
```

`i` is a `std::size_t` because `.size()` returns that unsigned type. Compare an `int` with it instead and `g++ -Wall` warns about a signed and unsigned comparison, because a negative `int` would turn into a huge unsigned number.

To print a list with separators *between* items, print the separator before every item except the first: `if (i > 0)`. Add `i + 1` when people expect numbering from 1.

The same loop can collect results. Keep a running total declared before the loop. For a running maximum, start from `INT_MIN` (from `<climits>`), the smallest possible `int`, so no element can lose to it. For a minimum, start from `INT_MAX`. Starting from `v[0]` also works, but only when the vector is not empty.

> Gotcha: a `std::size_t` is never negative, so a countdown loop with `i >= 0` never ends. After 0, `--i` wraps around to a huge number and `v[i]` reads far past the end. The next lesson shows the safe way to count down.

example.code:
#include <climits>
#include <cstddef>
#include <iostream>
#include <string>
#include <vector>

int main() {
    const std::vector<std::string> fruits = {"apple", "banana", "cherry"};
    for (std::size_t i = 0; i < fruits.size(); ++i) {
        std::cout << i << ": " << fruits[i] << '\n';
    }

    const std::vector<int> prices = {12, 45, 7, 30};
    int total = 0;
    int maxPrice = INT_MIN;
    for (std::size_t i = 0; i < prices.size(); ++i) {
        if (i > 0) {
            std::cout << ", ";
        }
        std::cout << prices[i];
        total += prices[i];
        if (prices[i] > maxPrice) {
            maxPrice = prices[i];
        }
    }
    std::cout << '\n';
    std::cout << "Total: " << total << '\n';
    std::cout << "Max: " << maxPrice << '\n';
    return 0;
}

