    double total = 0.0;
    for (double lap : laps) {
        total += lap;
    }
    std::cout << "Average: " << total / laps.size() << '\n';
    return 0;
}

steps.body:
A `while` loop with your own index fits when the loop should stop *early*, as soon as it finds what it wants. Put both stopping reasons in the condition:

```cpp
std::size_t i = 0;
while (i < v.size() && v[i] != target) {
    ++i;
}
// i is the match, or v.size() when nothing matched
```

Order matters. `&&` short-circuits: when `i < v.size()` is false, C++ never evaluates `v[i]`. Write the checks the other way round and, when nothing matches, the final test reads `v[v.size()]`, one past the end, which is undefined behavior.

After the loop, `i < v.size()` tells you whether it stopped on a match or ran off the end. `i` also counts how many elements were passed, which is handy for "take items until" problems.

You can also stop from inside the body with `break`, as in any loop:

```cpp
while (i < v.size()) {
    if (v[i] == target) {
        break;
    }
    ++i;
}
```

example.code:
#include <cstddef>
#include <iostream>
#include <vector>

int main() {
    const std::vector<int> values = {4, 8, 15, -16, 23};
    std::size_t i = 0;
    while (i < values.size() && values[i] >= 0) {
        ++i;
    }
    if (i < values.size()) {
        std::cout << "First negative at index " << i << '\n';
    } else {
        std::cout << "No negatives\n";
    }
    return 0;
}

